    "description": "The Inventory platform persists more than sixty domain classes, including Product, Warehouse, StockMovement, Supplier, and PurchaseOrder. Each class contains state and business behavior but no SQL, save methods, or storage-related annotations. Field-to-column declarations live in externally supplied YAML files that identify tables, columns, generated identifiers, null handling, and enum conversions; a single MappingEngine reads and writes every class from those declarations. The build also generates descriptor files from an existing PostgreSQL schema so that production can remain schema-first, and deployment-specific descriptor sets may target different column layouts without recompiling domain code. A schema change must require editing a declaration or regenerating descriptors, not editing every affected class, and domain unit tests must be able to construct entities without the mapping engine or a database.",
    "decision_statement": "Should field-to-column correspondence and conversion rules be held in external declarations consumed by one generic engine, keeping persistence details out of the domain classes?",
    "notes": "External YAML declarations plus one generic engine map sixty behavior-rich classes without per-class persistence code; lightweight-entity is the near miss framing the same entities as persistence-ignorant."
  },
